CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Act is to contribute to the safety and welfare of the public by establishing matters necessary to secure the safety of buildings and maintain and improve their use value such as convenience, comfort, aesthetics, and functionality, and matters necessary to safely dismantle buildings, and by managing buildings scientifically and systematically throughout their life cycle.

Article 2 (Definitions) The terms used in this Act shall be as follows:
  1. The term "building" means a building under Article 2 (1) 2 of the Building Act; provided, a building falling under any of the subparagraphs of Article 3 (1) of the Building Act shall be excluded;
  2. The term "building management" means the maintenance, inspection, repair, reinforcement, or dismantlement of a building by a manager until the building is destroyed;
  3. The term "manager" means a person designated as a manager of the relevant building under relevant statutes or regulations or the owner of the relevant building; in such cases, a person who is responsible for the management of the building pursuant to a management contract, etc. with the owner of the relevant building shall be deemed a manager;
  4. The term "life cycle information" means document information, drawing information, etc. produced during the life cycle of a building, such as planning, design, construction, maintenance, and destruction of a building;
  5. The term "building management plan" means a plan formulated pursuant to Article 11 to ensure the safety of buildings and to maintain and improve their use value;
  6. The term "reinforcement of fire safety performance" means all acts to improve the safety performance of a building in case of a fire through reinforcement of fire safety facilities and equipment, such as replacement of finishing materials, supplementation of fire compartments, and installation of fire extinguishing equipment, such as sprinklers, with respect to a building for which approval for use under Article 22 of the Building Act (hereinafter referred to as "approval for use") has been granted;
  7. The term "dismantlement" means destroying or cutting and removing all or part of a building to construct, substantially repair, remodel, or destroy a building;
  8. The term "destruction" means a state in which a building has completely lost its utility and shape due to dismantlement, deterioration, disaster, etc.

Article 3 (Responsibilities of the State and local governments) (1) The State and local governments shall formulate comprehensive policy measures on building management, such as the improvement of building management technology, the promotion of related industries, and the safety of buildings, and shall formulate administrative and financial supportive measures necessary therefor. <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  (2) The State and local governments shall endeavor to promote necessary education and publicity campaigns to raise people's awareness of building management.

Article 4 (Obligations of managers) (1) A manager shall endeavor to preserve and improve the functions of buildings and to enhance the convenience and safety of users.
  (2) A manager shall endeavor to secure financial resources necessary for the management of buildings under his or her jurisdiction each year.
  (3) A manager or lessee shall actively cooperate with the State and local governments in activities for the safety and maintenance of buildings.
  (4) A lessee shall actively cooperate with the duties of a manager.

Article 5 (Relationship to other statutes) Except as provided in other statutes, building management shall be governed by this Act.

CHAPTER II ESTABLISHMENT OF FOUNDATION FOR BUILDING MANAGEMENT

Article 6 (Fact-finding surveys) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu (referring to the head of an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply) may conduct a fact-finding survey on the following matters to secure basic data necessary for formulating and implementing policies on building management; in such cases, in receipt of a request from the heads of relevant central administrative agencies, he or she may conduct a joint fact-finding survey:
  1. Current status of buildings by use and size;
  2. Current status of application of seismic design and seismic capacity of buildings;
  3. Current status of fire safety performance and reinforcement of buildings;
  4. Current status of maintenance of buildings;
  5. Other matters requiring investigations to formulate policies on building management.
  (2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may request the head of a central administrative agency, the head of a local government, the head of a public institution under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions (hereinafter referred to as "public institution"), or the manager, who are related to building management, to submit materials necessary for conducting a fact-finding survey under paragraph (1). In such cases, a person in receipt of a request for submission of materials shall comply therewith, unless there is a compelling reason not to do so.
  (3) Matters concerning the methods, etc. of conducting a fact-finding survey under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.

Article 7 (Establishment of building life cycle information system) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may establish a building life cycle information system to effectively maintain and manage buildings, including the following matters:
  1. Information related to building management under Article 10;
  2. Building management plans;
  3. Results of regular inspections under Article 13;
  4. Results of emergency inspections under Article 14;
  5. Results of inspections of small-scale deteriorated buildings, etc. under Article 15;
  6. Results of safety examinations under Article 16;
  7. Results of dismantlement works of a building under Article 33;
  8. Seismic capacity of buildings under Article 48-3 of the Building Act;
  9. Information on energy use and greenhouse gas emissions in buildings under Article 10 of the Green Buildings Construction Support Act.
  10. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) When the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport establishes a building life cycle information system under paragraph (1), he or she shall ensure that it can be linked with the electronic information processing system under Article 32 (1) of the Building Act.
  (3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may request a person who possesses or manages the following data or information to provide data or information necessary for the establishment and operation of the building life cycle information system; in such cases, a person in receipt of such request shall comply therewith, unless there is a compelling reason not to do so: <Amended on Mar. 31, 2020; Nov. 30, 2021>
  1. Information on safety and maintenance of establishments under Article 55 of the Special Act on the Safety Control and Maintenance of Establishments;
  2. Information on in-house inspection, etc. of firefighting systems, etc. under Article 22 of the Act on Installation and Management of Firefighting Systems;
  3. Information on sanitary measures under Article 33 of the Water Supply and Waterworks Installation Act;
  4. Information on inspections of installation of elevators and safety inspection thereof under Articles 28 and 32 of the Elevator Safety Management Act;
  5. Information on inspections of machinery and equipment subject to inspections under Article 39 of the Energy Use Rationalization Act;
  6. Information on inspections of electric installations for general use under Article 12 of the Electrical Safety Management Act;
  7. Information on operation and management of private sewerage treatment facilities under Article 39 of the Sewerage Act;
  8. Disaster Information established pursuant to Article 34 of the Countermeasures against Natural Disasters Act;
  9. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (4) Necessary matters such as the procedures for requesting information or data and methods of submission under paragraph (3) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.

Article 8 (Disclosure and utilization of building life cycle information) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may disclose building life cycle information to encourage appropriate building management by any of the following methods:
  1. The building life cycle information system established pursuant to Article 7 (1);
  2. The website operated by an institution or organization designated by a provider of information and communications services under Article 2 (1) 3 of the Act on Promotion of Information and Communications Network Utilization and Information Protection or by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (2) When a practicing licensed real estate agent under subparagraph 4 of Article 2 of the Licensed Real Estate Agents Act brokers a conveyance of a building, he or she may guide the parties to a transaction so that they can confirm the life cycle information of the building subject to brokerage.

Article 9 (Building life cycle management register) (1) A Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall prepare and keep information on the current status of building management in the building life management register to verify the current status of building management in any of the following cases:
  1. Where a regular inspection under Article 13 is conducted;
  2. Where an emergency inspection under Article 14 is conducted;
  3. Where an inspection of small-scale deteriorated buildings, etc. under Article 15 is conducted;
  4. Where a safety examination under Article 16 is conducted;
  5. Where dismantlement works of a building under Article 30 are conducted;
  6. Other cases prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) The form, descriptions, and procedure for recording in the building life cycle management register under paragraph (1) and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.

Article 10 (Keeping and provision of information on building management) (1) In any case falling under the subparagraphs of Article 9 (1) for systematic building management, a manager shall record, keep, and maintain information on building management, such as inspection, repair, and reinforcement of the relevant building, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, in order to systematically manage the relevant building.
  (2) If necessary to conduct a regular inspection under Article 13, an emergency inspection under Article 14, or a safety examination under Article 16, a manager may request a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu to provide information on building management, such as design documents and drawings of the relevant building. In such cases, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall provide the relevant information, unless there is a compelling reason not to do so.

CHAPTER Ⅲ BUILDING MANAGEMENT INSPECTION AND MEASURES THEREFOR

Article 11 (Establishment of building management plans) (1) Where a building for which approval for use is to be obtained is to be constructed by a constructor in accordance with Article 41 of the Framework Act on the Construction Industry, the owner of the relevant building shall formulate a building management plan and submit it to the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu at the time of filing an application for approval for use; provided, this shall not apply to any of the following buildings: <Amended on Apr. 30, 2019; Nov. 15, 2022>
  1. Facilities for animals and plants under Article 2 (2) 21 of the Building Act;
  2. Correctional facilities and military installations under Article 2 (2) 23 of the Building Act;
  3. Broadcasting and telecommunications facilities under Article 2 (2) 24 of the Building Act;
  4. Multi-family housing subject to compulsory management under Article 2 (1) 2 of the Multi-Family Housing Management Act;
  5. Other buildings prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) The building management plan under paragraph (1) shall be prepared including the following matters, and the detailed standards for preparing the building management plan shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport:
  1. Matters concerning the current state of buildings;
  2. Matters concerning clients, designers, contractors, and supervisors;
  3. Matters concerning finishing materials of a building and products attached thereto;
  4. Matters concerning long-term repair plans for buildings;
  5. Matters concerning fire and escape safety of a building;
  6. Matters concerning structural safety of buildings and seismic capacity;
  7. Matters concerning management of energy and environment-friendly performance;
  8. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) The Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may examine the appropriateness of a building management plan under paragraph (1) and request the client or manager of the relevant building to supplement the building management plan.
  (4) The Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may register the result of examination of appropriateness of a building management plan under paragraph (3) in the building life cycle information system under paragraph (7).
  (5) A manager shall examine a building management plan every 3 years, and where necessary, adjust such plan, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, and replace or repair important facilities in accordance with the building management plan formulated or adjusted.
  (6) Where a manager has adjusted a building management plan pursuant to paragraph (5) or where he or she repairs, alters, or extends major parts of a building, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, he or she shall enter the results of measures taken in the building life cycle information system under Article 7.
  (7) The Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may entrust the examination of appropriateness of a building management plan under paragraph (3) to an institution or organization prescribed by Presidential Decree or have such institution or organization perform such duties on his or her behalf.

Article 12 (Maintenance and management of buildings) (1) A manager shall manage buildings, sites, and building equipment in compliance with Articles 40 through 48, 48-4, 49, 50, 50-2, 51, 52, 52-2, 53, 53-2, 54 through 58, 60 through 62, 64, 65-2, 67, and 68 of the Building Act and Articles 15, 15-2, 16, and 17 of the Green Buildings Construction Support Act. In such cases, Articles 65-2 of the Building Act and Articles 16 and 17 of the Green Buildings Construction Support Act shall be limited to cases where certification is obtained.
  (2) In applying the provisions of paragraph (1) or Articles 13 through 15, buildings prescribed by Presidential Decree, among buildings with unique structures, materials, forms, construction methods, etc. may be applied with strengthened or modified methods, procedures, and inspection standards for building management, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 13 (Conducting regular inspections) (1) A manager of buildings prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as public-use buildings, shall conduct regular inspections to maintain the safety and functions of buildings.
  (2) A regular inspection shall be conducted on the items prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as site, height, shape, structural safety, fire safety, building service, energy and environment-friendly management, crime prevention, and formulation and implementation of a building management plan; provided, where reconstruction diagnosis under the Act on the Improvement of Urban Areas and Residential Environments, or a safety inspection or safety examination under the Multi-Family Housing Management Act, or the Special Act on the Safety Control and Maintenance of Establishments has been conducted in the relevant year, matters concerning structural safety may be omitted during the regular inspection. <Amended on Dec. 3, 2024>
  (3) A regular inspection under paragraph (1) shall be conducted for the first time within 5 years from the date of approval for use of the relevant building and shall be conducted every 3 years (referring to the period that ends on the day before the base date of every third year) from the date of commencement of the inspection.
  (4) Necessary matters concerning procedures, methods, etc. of conducting a regular inspection shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 14 (Conducting emergency inspections) (1) A Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall request the manager of the relevant building to inspect the structural safety, fire safety, etc. of the building in any of the following cases:
  1. Where an inspection is deemed necessary to ensure the safety of buildings from disasters, etc.;
  2. Where buildings are deemed vulnerable to safety due to serious deterioration;
  3. Other cases prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) An Inspection under paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as "emergency inspection") shall be conducted within 1 month from the date when a manager is requested to conduct an emergency inspection.
  (3) Necessary matters concerning items, procedures, methods, etc. of conducting an emergency inspection shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 15 (Conducting inspections of small-scale deteriorated buildings) (1) A Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may inspect the structural safety, fire safety, energy performance, etc. of buildings deemed vulnerable to safety or at risk of disaster, among the buildings falling under any of the following subparagraphs:
  1. Buildings with a size prescribed by ordinance among the buildings for which more than 30 years have passed since approval for use was granted;
  2. Facilities for senior citizens and children under Article 2 (2) 11 of the Building Act;
  3. Housing units for housing disadvantaged person under subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Act on the Support for Housing Disadvantaged Persons including Persons with Disabilities and the Aged;
  4. Other buildings prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) The Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall provide the relevant manager with the results of an inspection conducted under paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as "inspection of small-scale deteriorated buildings, etc.") and shall present measures, etc. to improve the results of the inspection.
  (3) The Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may fully or partially grant a subsidy or a loan for expenses which may incur in repair, reinforcement, etc. based on the inspection results of small-scale deteriorated buildings, etc., and may provide technical support necessary for repair, reinforcement, etc.
  (4) Necessary matters such as procedures and methods of conducting inspections on small-scale deteriorated buildings, etc. shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 16 (Conducting safety examination) (1) Where a manager deems it necessary to ensure the safety of a building bases on a result of a regular inspection under Article 13, an emergency inspection under Article 14, or an inspection of a small-scale deteriorated building, etc. under Article 15, he or she shall investigate, measure, and evaluate the causes, etc. of safety defects in the building and conduct an examination suggesting measures for repair, reinforcement, etc.
  (2) A Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may request the relevant manager to conduct an examination under paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as "safety examination") in any of the following cases; in such cases, a person in receipt of such request shall comply therewith, unless there is a compelling reason not to do so: <Amended on Jun. 9, 2020>
  1. Where a serious defect has occurred in a building;
  2. Where it is deemed that there is a risk of collapse, fall, etc. of a building;
  3. Where it is deemed necessary to conduct a safety examination to prevent disasters;
  4. Other cases prescribed by Presidential Decree as likely to infringe on public safety due to lower performance of a building.
  (3) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems it necessary to conduct a safety examination because the structure of a building is likely to significantly affect the safe use of the public, he or she may request a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu to conduct a safety examination, or may request an institution specializing in safety examinations registered pursuant to Article 28 (1) of the Special Act on the Safety Control and Maintenance of Establishments (hereinafter referred to as "institution specializing in safety examinations") or the Korea Authority of Land and Infrastructure Safety under the Act on the Korea Authority of Land and Infrastructure Safety (hereinafter referred to as the "Korea Authority of Land and Infrastructure Safety") to conduct a safety examination. <Amended on Jun. 9, 2020>
  (4) An institution specializing in safety examinations or the Korea Authority of Land and Infrastructure Safety which conducts a safety examination pursuant to paragraph (3) may ask interested persons necessary questions or inspect relevant documents, etc. <Amended on Jun. 9, 2020>
  (5) An institution specializing in safety examinations or the Korea Authority of Land and Infrastructure Safety which conducts a safety examination pursuant to paragraph (3) shall prepare a report on the results thereof, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, and submit it to the relevant manager, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu. <Amended on Jun. 9, 2020>
  (6) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu deems it necessary to take measures, such as repair and reinforcement, based on the results of the safety examination conducted under paragraph (3), he or she may order the relevant manager to take measures, such as repair and reinforcement.
  (7) Where the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu has conducted a safety examination pursuant to paragraph (3), he or she shall submit a report on the results thereof to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.

Article 17 (Building management inspection guidelines) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall prepare and publicly notify guidelines prescribing matters concerning the methods, procedures, etc. of conducting regular inspections, emergency inspections, inspections of small-scale deteriorated buildings, etc. and safety inspections (hereinafter referred to as "building management inspections") under Articles 13 through 16 (hereinafter referred to as "building management inspection guidelines").
  (2) When the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport establishes building management inspection guidelines, he or she shall consult in advance with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies.

Article 18 (Designation of building management inspection institution) (1) A Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall designate any of the following persons as a building management inspection institution and notify the relevant manager thereof, as prescribed by Presidential Decree: <Amended on Apr. 30, 2019; Jun. 9, 2020; Mar. 16, 2021>
  1. A person who has filed a report on the establishment of an architectural firm under Article 23 (1) of the Certified Architects Act;
  2. A construction engineering business entity registered pursuant to Article 26 (1) of the Construction Technology Promotion Act;
  3. An institution specializing in safety examinations;
  4. The Korea Authority of Land and Infrastructure Safety;
  5. Other persons prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) The manager shall have a building management inspection institution designated pursuant to paragraph (1) conduct a building management inspection.
  (3) The building management inspection institution shall designate a person in charge of inspection to perform its duties.
  (4) The inspector shall conscientiously perform his or her duties pursuant to the building management inspection guidelines.
  (5) The manager may request the replacement of a building management inspection institution in any of the following cases. In such cases, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall replace the building management inspection institution and notify the manager thereof, if he or she deems that there is good cause:
  1. Where it has been designated as a building management inspection institution by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where it fails to meet the qualification standards for inspectors required for building management inspections;
  3. Where the inspector has performed duties in violation of the building management inspection guidelines by intention or gross negligence;
  4. Where the building management inspection institution refuses or fails to conduct a building management inspection without good cause.
  (6) Necessary matters concerning qualifications of an inspector and considerations for services thereof, etc. shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 19 (Notification of building management inspection) (1) A Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall notify the manager of a building who shall conduct an inspection falling under any of the following subparagraphs of the fact that the building is subject to inspection and the procedures for conducting the inspection in advance by not later than 3 months before the date of such inspection; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 2, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall notify the manager of the building, without delay, of the fact that the building is subject to inspection and the procedures for conducting the inspection:
  1. Regular inspections under Article 13;
  2. Emergency inspections under Article 14;
  3. Inspections of small-scale deteriorated buildings, etc. under Article 15.
  (2) Methods of notification under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.

Article 20 (Reporting on results of building management inspection) (1) A building management inspection institution shall report the results of a building management inspection to the manager of the relevant building and the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu within 30 days from the date the building management inspection is completed.
  (2) When the building management inspection institution reports the results of a building management inspection under paragraph (1), it shall verify whether the following matters are implemented: <Amended on Mar. 31, 2020; Nov. 30, 2021>
  1. Safety inspections under Article 11 of the Special Act on the Safety Control and Maintenance of Establishments;
  2. In-house inspections, etc. of firefighting systems, etc. under Article 22 of the Act on Installation and Management of Firefighting Systems;
  3. Sanitary measures under Article 33 of the Water Supply and Waterworks Installation Act;
  4. Inspections of installation of elevators and safety inspections thereof under Articles 28 and 32 of the Elevator Safety Management Act;
  5. Inspections of machinery and equipment subject to inspections under Article 39 of the Energy Use Rationalization Act;
  6. Inspections of electric installations for general use under Article 12 of the Electrical Safety Management Act;
  7. Operation and management of private sewerage treatment facilities under Article 39 of the Sewerage Act;
  8. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) Reporting on the results of a building management inspection under paragraph (1) may be substituted by entering such results into the building life cycle information system under Article 7.

Article 21 (Restrictions on use) (1) In cases prescribed by Presidential Decree where it is deemed necessary to take urgent measures because it has a serious impact on the safe use of a building, a manager shall take measures, such as restrictions on use, prohibition of use, or dismantlement of the relevant building.
  (2) Where the manager takes measures under paragraph (1), he or she shall notify the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu of such fact in advance. In such cases, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, in receipt of such notification, shall publicly announce such fact.
  (3) The Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, in receipt of a report on the results of a building management inspection pursuant to Article 20 (1), may issue an order to take measures, such as restrictions on use, prohibition of use, or dismantlement of the relevant building, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, if it is deemed necessary to take urgent measures because it has a serious impact on the safe use of the relevant building.
  (4) Where a person in receipt of an order issued under paragraph (3) fails to comply with such order, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may conduct vicarious execution pursuant to the Administrative Vicarious Execution Act.

Article 22 (Implementation of inspection results) (1) When a manager receives a report on the results of a building management inspection pursuant to Article 20 (1), he or she shall take necessary measures, such as repair and reinforcement, against serious defects prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as seismic performance and fire safety performance, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Where the manager fails to take necessary measures, such as repair or reinforcement of the building under paragraph (1), the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may order the relevant manager to dismantle, alter, repair, prohibit or restrict the use thereof, or implement or take other necessary measures or corrective measures.
  (3) The manager who has been notified of the results of the building management inspection shall, where urgent repair, reinforcement, etc. of the building is required, notify the users, etc. of the relevant building thereof through broadcasting, Internet, signboards, etc.

Article 23 (Reporting on results of measures taken) (1) The manager who has completed necessary measures, such as repair and reinforcement, pursuant to Article 22 shall report the results thereof to the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu.
  (2) Matters concerning the procedures, etc. for reporting under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.

Article 24 (Evaluation on results of building management inspection) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, a Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Do Governor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, or a Special Self-Governing Province Governor may evaluate the results of a building management inspection, if necessary for improving the level of technology related to building management and preventing substandard inspections in buildings.
  (2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, the Metropolitan City Mayor, the Do Governor, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, or the Special Self-Governing Province Governor may request the manager or the building management inspection institution to submit data necessary for evaluation under paragraph (1). In such cases, a person in receipt of a request for submission of data shall comply therewith.
  (3) Where the building management inspection institution fails to faithfully conduct an inspection of building management based on the evaluation of the results of the building management inspection, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, the Metropolitan City Mayor, the Do Governor, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, or the Special Self-Governing Province Governor may order the building management inspection institution to make improvements by a specified deadline.
  (4) Matters necessary for the subjects, methods, and procedures of evaluation under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 25 (Suspension of business of building management inspection institution) (1) Where a building management inspection institution falls under any of the following cases, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may order the building management inspection institution to suspend its business for a specified period not exceeding 6 months or may impose a penalty surcharge not exceeding 100 million won, in lieu of the suspension of business:
  1. Where he or she falls under any of the subparagraphs of Article 18 (5);
  2. Where it is recognized based on the evaluation of the results of the building management inspection conducted under Article 24 that the building management inspection has been falsely or poorly conducted;
  3. Where the results of the building management inspection are falsely entered in the building life cycle information system under Article 7.
  (2) Where a person subject to the imposition of a penalty surcharge under paragraph (1) fails to pay the penalty surcharge by the deadline, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall collect it pursuant to the Act on the Collection of Local Administrative Penalty Charges. <Amended on Mar. 24, 2020>
  (3) Matters concerning the standards for the disposition of the suspension of business under paragraph (1) and the amounts, etc. of penalty surcharges based on types of violations on which penalty surcharges are imposed and the level of violation shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 26 (Expense bearing) (1) Expenses incurred in conducting building management inspections shall be borne by the relevant manager; provided, expenses incurred in conducting inspections for small-scale deteriorated buildings, etc. under Article 15 shall be borne by the relevant Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of the relevant Si/Gun/Gu.
  (2) Where the manager is unable to conduct a building management inspection due to unavoidable causes, such as insolvency resulting from inability to pay bills or checks, the competent Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu may conduct the inspection on behalf of the relevant manager. In such cases, the relevant manager may be required to bear expenses incurred in conducting the inspection on his or her behalf.
  (3) Where the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu requests the relevant manager to bear expenses after conducting the building management inspection on his or her pursuant to paragraph (2), if the relevant manager fails to comply with such request, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of the Si/Gun/Gu may collect such expenses in the same manner as delinquent local taxes are collected.

Article 27 (Reinforcement of fire safety performance of existing buildings) (1) A manager shall endeavor to continuously maintain the fire safety performance of buildings in order to ensure public safety from fires.
  (2) The manager of a building (hereinafter referred to as "building subject to reinforcement") having at least 3 floors and meeting the requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as the total floor area, use, and finishing materials, among any of the following buildings, who has filed an application for building permission under Article 11 of the Building Act [including cases where an application for deliberation by a building committee under Article 4 of the Building Act (hereinafter referred to as "building committee") is filed pursuant to Article 4-2 of the same Act and a report on building construction under Article 14 of the same Act is filed] before this Act enters into force, shall reinforce fire safety performance pursuant to Article 28:
  1. Class 1 neighborhood living facilities under Article 2 (2) 3 of the Building Act;
  2. Class 2 neighborhood living facilities under Article 2 (2) 4 of the Building Act;
  3. Medical facilities under Article 2 (2) 9 of the Building Act;
  4. Education and research facilities under Article 2 (2) 10 of the Building Act;
  5. Facilities for senior citizens and children under Article 2 (2) 11 of the Building Act;
  6. Training facilities under Article 2 (2) 12 of the Building Act;
  7. Lodging facilities under Article 2 (2) 15 of the Building Act.
  (3) The Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall notify the manager of the building subject to reinforcement of the building that it is subject to reinforcement of fire safety performance. In such cases, a person who has an objection to such notice may file an objection, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Apr. 18, 2023>
  (4) Notwithstanding paragraph (2), where a management and disposal plan for an improvement project under the Act on the Improvement of Urban Areas and Residential Environments or a project implementation plan for a small-scale housing improvement project under the Act on Special Cases concerning Unoccupied House or Small-Scale Housing Improvement is authorized or is not used for the purpose of reinforcement due to business closure, such buildings shall be excluded from the buildings subject to reinforcement. <Added on Apr. 18, 2023>

Article 28 (Implementation of reinforcement of fire safety performance) (1) The manager of the building subject to reinforcement shall formulate a plan for the reinforcement of fire safety performance, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, and submit it to the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu to obtain approval for such plan.
  (2) Where the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu intends to approve a plan for the reinforcement of fire safety performance under paragraph (1), he or she shall undergo deliberation by the building committee.
  (3) The manager of the building subject to reinforcement shall conduct reinforcement pursuant to the plan under paragraph (1) and report the results thereof to the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu by December 31, 2025. <Amended on Apr. 18, 2023>
  (4) Where the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu receives a report on the results under paragraph (3), he or she shall review the report and register the results thereof in the building life cycle information system under paragraph (7).
  (5) Where the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu deems it necessary to supplement the reinforcement of fire safety performance as a result of an inspection conducted under paragraph (4), he or she may issue an order to supplement such fire safety performance by a specified deadline.
  (6) The manager of the building subject to reinforcement who has received an order to supplement under paragraph (5) shall supplement the reinforcement of fire safety performance by the specified deadline and report the results thereof to the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu. <Amended on Jun. 9, 2020>
  (7) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall determine and publicly notify detailed matters concerning the methods and standards of reinforcement of buildings subject to reinforcement, such as replacement of finishing materials, installation of evacuation facilities and fire extinguishing equipment.

Article 29 Deleted. <Apr. 18, 2023>

Article 29-2 (Support for reinforcement of fire safety performance) (1) The State or local governments may provide managers with technical support or information necessary for formulating a plan for the reinforcement of fire safety performance under Article 28 (1).
  (2) The State and local governments shall subsidize expenses incurred in the reinforcement of fire safety performance of buildings subject to reinforcement, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Apr. 18, 2023]
  [The amended provisions of Article 29-2 (2) of the Act (Act No. 19367, Apr. 18, 2023) shall be effective until December 31, 2025 pursuant to the provisions of Article 2 of the Addenda to that Act]

CHAPTER IV DISMANTLEMENT AND DISTRUCTION OF BUILDING SITES

Article 30 (Permission for dismantlement of buildings) (1) Where a manager intends to dismantle a building, he or she shall obtain permission from the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu (hereafter referred to as "permitting authority" in this Chapter); provided, in any of the following cases, he or she shall be deemed to have obtained permission if he or she files a report, as prescribed by Presidential Decree: <Amended on Apr. 7, 2020>
  1. Where a part of a building is dismantled without accompanying the dismantlement of main structural members under Article 2 (1) 7 of the Building Act;
  2. Where all of the following buildings are dismantled:
    (a) A building with a total floor area of less than 500 square meters;
    (b) A building with a height of less than 12 meters;
    (c) A building with less than 3 floors, including ground floors and underground floors;
  3. Other cases of dismantlement of a building prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Notwithstanding the proviso, with the exception of its subparagraphs, of paragraph (1), where the manager intends to dismantle a building in any of the following cases, he or she shall obtain permission from the permitting authority: <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  1. Where there are facilities prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government, such as bus stops, entrances to urban railroad stations, and crosswalks, within a certain radius of the area around the relevant building;
  2. Where there exists a road with at least the width prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government within the extent corresponding to the height of the building from the exterior walls of the relevant building;
  3. Other cases prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government in consideration of the surrounding conditions of the relevant building, such as the arrangement of the building and floating population, for safe dismantlement of the building.
  (3) A person who intends to obtain permission or make a report pursuant to paragraph (1) or (2) shall submit an application for permission for dismantlement of a building or a report thereon to the competent permitting authority, accompanied with a dismantlement plan prepared pursuant to paragraph (4) or examined pursuant to paragraph (5). <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  (4) The dismantlement plan submitted by the person who intends to obtain permission pursuant to the main clause, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of paragraph (1) or paragraph (2) to the competent permitting authority shall be prepared by any of the following persons in compliance with this Act, orders or dispositions issued under this Act, and other relevant statutes and regulations, and shall be affixed with his or her signature and sealed thereon: <Added on Feb. 3, 2022>
  1. A person who has filed a report on the establishment of an architectural firm under Article 23 (1) of the Certified Architects Act;
  2. A person who has registered the establishment of a professional engineer office pursuant to Article 6 of the Professional Engineers Act and has registered the scope of duties prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as building structure.
  (5) The dismantlement plan submitted by the person who intends to file a report pursuant to the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of paragraph (1) to the competent permitting authority shall be examined by any of the following persons in compliance with this Act, orders or dispositions issued under this Act, and other relevant statutes and regulations, and shall be affixed with his or her signature and sealed thereon: <Added on Feb. 3, 2022>
  1. A person who has filed a report on the establishment of an architectural firm under Article 23 (1) of the Certified Architects Act;
  2. A person who has registered the establishment of a professional engineer office pursuant to Article 6 of the Professional Engineers Act and has registered the scope of duties prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as building structure.
  (6) The permitting authority shall determine whether to grant permission to dismantle the relevant building or to accept a report on dismantlement thereof, subject to deliberation by the building committee established thereby pursuant to Article 4 (1) of the Building Act, in any of the following cases: <Added on Feb. 3, 2022>
  1. Where it is intended to permit the dismantlement of a building under the main clause of paragraph (1), with the exception of its subparagraphs, or paragraph (2);
  2. Where a report on dismantlement of a building is received pursuant to the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of paragraph (1) and the permitting authority deems it necessary to conduct a specialized examination for the safe management of dismantlement of the building.
  (7) Where it is deemed necessary to supplement a dismantlement plan, etc. based on the result of deliberation under paragraph (6) or the judgment of the permitting authority, the permitting authority shall request the manager to supplement it within a specified period, and the manager shall comply therewith unless there is good cause. <Added on Feb. 3, 2022>
  (8) The permitting authority shall request the Korea Authority of Land and Infrastructure Safety to examine a plan for dismantlement of a building prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Jun. 9, 2020; Feb. 3, 2022>
  (9) The methods of preparing and examining a dismantlement plan under the provisions of paragraphs (3) through (5), the details thereof, the procedures for granting permission to dismantle a building, and other matters shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>

Article 30-2 (Report on commencement of dismantlement works) (1) The manager who intends to commence dismantlement works of a building permitted to be dismantled pursuant to the main clause, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 30 (1) or paragraph (2) of the same Article shall file a report on the commencement of dismantlement works with the permitting authority, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, this shall not apply to buildings for which a report has been filed pursuant to the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 30 (1). <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  (2) The permitting authority shall notify a person who has submitted a report, of whether his or her report is accepted or whether the processing period prescribed by statutes or regulations related to civil petitions is extended, within 7 days from the date the report under paragraph (1) is received. <Added on Feb. 3, 2022>
  (3) If the permitting authority fails to notify the person who has submitted a report of whether his or her report is accepted or whether the processing period prescribed by statutes or regulations related to civil petitions is extended within the period prescribed in paragraph (2), the report shall be deemed accepted on the day following the last day of the processing period.
  [This Article Added on Jul. 27, 2021]
  [Moved from Article 30-3; previous Article 30-2 moved to Article 30-4 <Feb. 3, 2022>]

Article 30-3 (Modification of matters permitted or reported for dismantlement of buildings) (1) Where a manager intends to modify matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as applying dismantlement methods different from the methods specified in the dismantlement plan, among the matters permitted or reported pursuant to Article 30 (1) or (2), he or she shall obtain permission for modification thereof from the permitting authority or file a report on modification thereof with the permitting authority, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. In such cases, Article 30 (3) through (7) and (9) shall apply mutatis mutandis to matters concerning the modification, etc. of the dismantlement plan.
  (2) Where the manager intends to modify matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as the replacement of a dismantlement worker under Article 32-2, among the matters reported on the commencement of dismantlement works pursuant to Article 30-2 (1), he or she shall report such modification to the permitting authority, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (3) Where the manager has modified any matter other than the matters subject to permission for or reporting on modification under paragraph (1) or (2), he or she shall file a report on modification with the permitting authority, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, at the time of filing a report on the completion of dismantlement works of a building under Article 33.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 3, 2022]
  [Previous Article 30-3 moved to Article 30-2 <Feb. 3, 2022>]

Article 30-4 (On-site inspections) (1) In cases prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as where the permitting authority receives a report on the commencement of dismantlement works under Article 30-2 for the prevention of accidents, etc., he or she shall conduct an on-site inspection of the building dismantlement site. <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  (2) Where it is found, as a result of an on-site inspection conducted under paragraph (1), that it is difficult to safely conduct dismantlement works, the permitting authority shall immediately order the manager, the supervisor of dismantlement works under Article 31 (1), dismantling workers under Article 32-2, etc. to take necessary measures, such as suspension of such works, and the person in receipt of such order shall take necessary measures, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  (3) The permitting authority shall confirm whether necessary measures under paragraph (2) have been taken as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport and then order the resumption of dismantlement works, etc., and if necessary measures have not been taken, he or she shall not order the resumption of dismantlement works, etc. <Added on Feb. 3, 2022>
  (4) The permitting authority may require the building management inspection institution under Article 18 (1) to conduct an on-site inspection under paragraph (1) on his or her behalf. In such cases, the person who conducts such inspection on his or her behalf shall report the results of the on-site inspection in writing to the permitting authority, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, and shall immediately take safety measures and then report such fact to the permitting authority if any matter to be taken urgently is found in the course of conducting the on-site inspection. <Added on Feb. 3, 2022>
  (5) Where the permitting authority allows a person to perform duties on his or her behalf pursuant to paragraph (4), he or she shall pay a fee prescribed by ordinance of the competent local government within the extent prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  [This Article Added on Apr. 7, 2020]
  [Moved from Article 30-2 <Feb. 3, 2022>]

Article 31 (Designation of supervisors of dismantlement works of buildings) (1) The permitting authority shall designate a person who has completed education on the supervision of dismantlement works under Article 31-2, among persons qualified as supervisors under the Certified Architects Act or the Construction Technology Promotion Act (excluding the contractor himself or herself and an affiliate company under subparagraph 12 of Article 2 of the Monopoly Regulation and Fair Trade Act), as a supervisor of dismantlement works (hereinafter referred to as "supervisor of dismantlement works"), as prescribed by Presidential Decree, and have the relevant person supervise dismantlement works to safely manage dismantlement works for buildings for which permission to dismantle buildings have been granted. <Amended on Dec. 29, 2020; Feb. 3, 2022>
  (2) The permitting authority shall replace the supervisor of dismantlement works in any of the following cases: <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  1. Where he or she has submitted documents concerning the designation of the supervisor of dismantlement works by fraud or improper means;
  2. Where he or she fails to request correction or suspension of dismantlement works even though he or she has been aware that the relevant manager or dismantlement worker has committed a violation under Article 32-2 while performing his or her duties;
  3. Notwithstanding an order for registration under Article 32 (7), where he or she continuously fails to comply with such order without good cause;
  4. Other cases prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) A supervisor of dismantlement works shall perform supervisory duties at the site of dismantlement works at any time or when necessary; provided, in cases of supervising dismantlement works of buildings prescribed by Presidential Decree in consideration of the methods, scope, etc. of dismantlement works, a person with qualifications or experience prescribed by Presidential Decree shall be assigned as a construction inspector to perform supervisory duties at the site of dismantlement works during the entire period of dismantlement works. <Added on Jun. 10, 2022>
  (4) The permitting authority shall restrict the designation of a supervisor of dismantlement works falling under any subparagraph of paragraph (2) for a period not exceeding 1 year. <Added on Feb. 3, 2022; Jun. 10, 2022>
  (5) Except as provided in this Act, the details and scope of responsibilities between a manager and a supervisor of dismantlement works shall be prescribed by a contract between the parties. <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022; Jun. 10, 2022>
  (6) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall determine the standards for the placement of construction inspectors under the proviso of paragraph (3), as prescribed by Presidential Decree. In such cases, the manager and the supervisor of dismantlement works shall comply therewith, unless there is good cause. <Added on Jul. 27, 2021; Feb. 3, 2022; Jun. 10, 2022>
  (7) Necessary matters such as standards for the designation of a supervisor of dismantlement works, methods of designation thereof, and supervision expenses for dismantlement works shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Jul. 27, 2021; Feb. 3, 2022; Jun. 10, 2022>

Article 31-2 (Education of supervisors of dismantlement works) (1) A supervisor of dismantlement works and a construction inspector who intend to conduct the supervision of dismantlement works shall receive education on the supervision of dismantlement works.
  (2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may designate an educational institution for dismantlement works, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, for smooth implementation of education under paragraph (1).
  (3) The educational institution for dismantlement works designated pursuant to paragraph (2) may provide education necessary for dismantlement works, such as the preparation and examination of a dismantlement plan, in addition to the supervision of dismantlement works, and the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may provide administrative and financial support necessary for the educational institution for dismantlement works to implement education thereon.
  (4) Matters necessary for methods, standards, and procedures of education under paragraphs (1) and (3) and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 3, 2022]

Article 32 (Duties of supervisors of dismantlement works) (1) A supervisor of dismantlement works shall perform the following duties: <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  1. Verification as to whether dismantlement works are conducted in compliance with the dismantlement plan under Article 30 (3) (where the details of the dismantlement plan are modified according to permission for modification or a report on modification under Article 30-3 (1), referring to the modified dismantlement plan; hereinafter referred to as "dismantlement plan"), which prescribe the order of dismantlement works, dismantlement methods, etc.;
  2. Verification as to whether dismantlement works are conducted in compliance with safety management measures, such as preventing fire and collapse, securing traffic safety and safety passages, and preventing plunges and falls at the site;
  3. Verification as to whether finishing works are implemented, such as clean-up of the site after dismantlement works, and repair and compensation of the neighboring environment;
  4. Verification as to whether construction wastes under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Construction Waste Recycling Promotion Act, which are generated by dismantlement works, are properly treated;
  5. Other duties concerning the supervision of dismantlement works determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (2) Where it is impracticable for the supervisor of dismantlement works to safely conduct dismantlement works of a building, he or she shall request the manager and the dismantlement worker under Article 32-2 to correct or suspend the dismantlement works, and the manager and the dismantlement worker shall comply therewith unless there is a compelling reason not to do so. <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  (3) Where the manager or the dismantlement worker under Article 32-2 continues the dismantlement works even after receiving a request for correction or suspension thereof under paragraph (2), the supervisor of dismantlement works shall report thereon to the competent permitting authority, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. Upon receipt of such report, the permitting authority shall order the competent permitting authority to suspend dismantlement works without delay. <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  (4) Where the manager or the dismantlement worker under Article 32-2 has complied with the request to take measures under paragraph (2) or where he or she intends to resume dismantlement works after receiving an order to suspend work under the latter part of paragraph (3), he or she shall submit an improvement plan necessary for ensuring the safety of buildings to the competent permitting authority for approval. <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  (5) In order to enable the permitting authority, etc. to verify whether the dismantlement of a building has been conducted in compliance with the dismantlement plan, the supervisor of dismantlement works shall take and record photographs and videos (referring to photographs and videos on which the date of taking photographs or recording videos is indicated) of the site where the relevant works are in progress in any of the following dismantlement works and store them: <Added on Feb. 3, 2022>
  1. Dismantlement works of hold points (referring to points in construction requiring approval from on-site inspections conducted by the supervisor of dismantlement works before proceeding to the following stage of construction works); in such cases, matters necessary for detailed standards, etc. of hold points shall prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  2. Dismantlement works deemed by the supervisor of dismantlement works to be significant.
  (6) The supervisor of dismantlement works shall daily register the following matters with respect to the dismantlement works conducted on the current day in the building life cycle information system under Article 7: <Added on Feb. 3, 2022>
  1. Types of works, details of supervision, matters pointed out, and results of processing such matters;
  2. Current status of safety inspection records;
  3. Matters specific at the site (details of situations, measures taken, etc.);
  4. Matters deemed necessary by supervisors of dismantlement works for site management records.
  (7) The permitting authority shall order the supervisor of dismantlement works who fails to register matters falling under the subparagraphs of paragraph (6) to register such matters, and the supervisor of dismantlement works shall comply therewith unless there is good cause. <Added on Feb. 3, 2022>
  (8) Where the dismantlement of a building is completed, the supervisor of dismantlement works shall submit a report on the completion of supervision of dismantlement works (including submission with a document in electronic form) to the relevant manager and the permitting authority. <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  (9) Matters necessary for the approval of an improvement plan under paragraph (4), the photograph-taking and video-recording and storage thereof under paragraph (5), the preparation of a report on completion of supervision of dismantlement works under paragraph (8), etc. shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>

Article 32-2 (Duties of dismantling workers) Dismantling workers shall perform the following duties:
  1. Performance of dismantlement works according to dismantlement plans;
  2. Implementation of safety management measures, such as measures for preventing fire and collapse, measures for securing traffic safety and safety passages, and measures for preventing plunges and falls;
  3. Duties prescribed by relevant statutes or regulations, such as the Occupational Safety and Health Act.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 3, 2022]

Article 33 (Report on completion of dismantlement works of buildings) (1) A manager shall file a report on the completion of dismantlement works of a building to the permitting authority within 30 days from any of the following dates: <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  1. In cases of a building subject to permission for dismantlement works under the main clause, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 30 (1) or paragraph (2) of the same Article, the date on a report on the completion of supervision of dismantlement works under Article 32 (8) is received from the supervisor of dismantlement works;
  2. In cases of a building subject to filing a report on dismantlement works under the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 30 (1), the date on which the building is dismantled and waste is shipped out of the building.
  (2) Matters concerning the methods and procedures of filing a report under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.

Article 34 (Report on destruction of buildings) (1) A manager shall submit a report on the destruction of a building to the competent permitting authority within 30 days from the date the relevant building is destroyed; provided, where the building is completely dismantled and a report on the completion of dismantlement works of the building is filed under Article 33, the report on the destruction of the building shall be deemed to have been filed. <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  (2) Matters concerning the methods and procedures of filing a report under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.

CHAPTER Ⅴ SUPPORT FOR BUILDING MANAGEMENT

Article 35 (Research and development of building management) (1) The Government may conduct research and development projects for building management technology by entering into an agreement with an institution or organization prescribed by Presidential Decree in order to promote policy measures for the improvement of building management technology and the promotion of related industries.
  (2) Expenses incurred in conducting research and development projects for building management technology under paragraph (1) shall be appropriated from contributions from the Government or any person other than the Government or technology development expenses of other enterprises.
  (3) Where it is deemed necessary to promote the use and dissemination of research and development results achieved pursuant to paragraph (1), and the linkage with related industries, the Government may conduct a pilot project for promoting the building management, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (4) Matters necessary for the methods for concluding agreements under paragraph (1) and the payment and use of contributions under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 36 (Fostering of engineers for building management) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may formulate and implement policy measures for fostering, educating, training, etc. of engineers for building management in order to efficiently utilize engineers for building management and improve their technical capabilities.
  (2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may require public institutions or institutions or organizations related to building management technology to provide education and training under paragraph (1) on his or her behalf in order to foster engineers related to building management. In such cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may partially subsidize expenses incurred in providing such education and training.
  (3) Matters necessary for details, methods, etc. of education and training under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 37 (Support for business operators related to building management) (1) The State or local governments may provide administrative and financial assistance to relevant business operators to facilitate the development of building management industries.
  (2) The scope of business operators eligible for assistance under paragraph (1), procedures for providing assistance, and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 38 (International exchange and cooperation) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may promote the following projects to facilitate international cooperation and overseas expansion of building management technology:
  1. Surveys and studies for international cooperation;
  2. International exchange of personnel and information;
  3. Joint research and development on building management technology conducted with foreign universities, research institutes, and organizations;
  4. Expansion into overseas markets by utilizing developed building management technologies;
  5. Other matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport to promote international exchanges and cooperation for the development of building management technologies.

Article 39 (Designation of building management support centers) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may designate the following institutions as building management support centers in order to efficiently promote the research, development, dissemination, etc. of policies and technologies for building management: <Amended on May 19, 2020; Jun. 9, 2020>
  1. The Architecture and Urban Research Institute established pursuant to the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutes;
  2. The Korea Authority of Land and Infrastructure Safety;
  3. The Korea Institute of Construction Technology established pursuant to the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Science and Technology Research Institutes;
  4. The Korea Real Estate Board under the Korea Real Estate Board Act;
  5. The Korea Land and Housing Corporation established pursuant to the Korea Land and Housing Corporation Act;
  6. Other public institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport designates building management support centers under paragraph (1) or revokes the designation thereof, he or she shall publicly notify such fact in the Official Gazette.
  (3) Building management support centers under paragraph (1) shall perform the following duties:
  1. Support for formulation and implementation of policies related to building management;
  2. Support for consulting on building management;
  3. Duties executed vicariously or entrusted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport pursuant to this Act;
  4. Other duties necessary for systematic management of buildings.
  (4) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may contribute or subsidize part of the expenses necessary for building management support centers designated pursuant to paragraph (1) in implementing duties under paragraph (3) within the budget.
  (5) Matters necessary for the designation as building management support centers, the revocation of designation thereof, etc. under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 40 (Establishment and operation of local building management support centers) (1) A Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may provide managers with technical support or information so that they can efficiently manage buildings pursuant to a building management plan.
  (2) Where necessary for technical assistance, provision of information, formulation of safety measures, etc. under paragraph (1), a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may establish and operate a local building management support center.
  (3) Local building management support centers under paragraph (2) may be operated by integrating them with the local construction safety centers under Article 87-2 (1) of the Building Act.
  (4) Matters necessary for the establishment, operation, etc. of local building management support centers under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.

CHAPTER VI SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 41 (Corrective orders for buildings) (1) Where a building falls under any of the following cases, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may dismantle, alter, extend, repair, prohibit or restrict the use of, the building, or take other necessary measures:
  1. Where a building is located in a military base and installation protection zone under subparagraph 6 of Article 2 of the Protection of Military Bases and Installations Act and is requested by the Minister of National Defense due to national security needs;
  2. Where a building is deemed highly likely to cause harm to multiple people by collapse or fire, in violation of Articles 40 through 48, 50, or 52 of the Building Act, as a result of deliberation by a local building committee under Article 72 (2) of the Building Act;
  3. Other cases prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Where a building is located in a scenic district under Article 37 (1) 1 of the National Land Planning and Utilization and is deemed to seriously disrupt urban aesthetics or residential environment, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may require the building committee to alter, repair, or take other necessary measures after hearing the opinions of the building committee.
  (3) Where the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu issues an order to take necessary measures pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall pay reasonable compensation, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 42 (Improvement of unoccupied buildings) Where a building (excluding unoccupied houses under subparagraph 12 of Article 2 of the Agricultural and Fishing Villages Improvement Act and unoccupied houses under Article 2 (1) 1 of the Act on Special Cases concerning Unoccupied House or Small-Scale Housing Improvement; hereinafter referred to as "unoccupied building") not used by no one for at least 1 year falls under any of the following subparagraphs, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may order the owner of the building to take necessary measures, such as dismantlement, after deliberation by the building committee; in such cases, the owner of the building shall comply therewith within 60 days unless there is a compelling reason not to do so:
  1. Where it is deemed to be harmful to the public interest or to seriously disrupt urban aesthetics or residential environment;
  2. Where it is necessary to expand fundamental infrastructure and common facilities under subparagraphs 4 and 5 of Article 2 of the Act on the Improvement of Urban Areas and Residential Environments to improve the residential or urban environment.

Article 43 (Procedures for improvement of unoccupied buildings) (1) Where a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu has issued an order to dismantle an unoccupied building pursuant to Article 42, and the owner of the unoccupied building fails to comply with such order without any special reason, he or she may dismantle the relevant building ex officio, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) If the whereabouts of the owner of an unoccupied building to be dismantled pursuant to paragraph (1) is unknown, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall publicly announce it, at least once, in a daily newspaper, and if its owner fails to dismantle it within 60 days from the date of public announcement in the daily newspaper, he or she may dismantle the building ex officio.
  (3) In cases falling under paragraphs (1) and (2), the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall pay reasonable compensation to the owner of the unoccupied building, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. In such cases, if the owner of the unoccupied building refuses to receive the compensation or if it is impossible to pay the compensation due to the unknown whereabouts of the owner of the unoccupied building, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of the Si/Gun/Gu shall deposit such compensation.
  (4) When dismantling the unoccupied building pursuant to paragraph (1) or (2), the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall reflect such fact in the Building Register without delay, notify the competent registry office that the unoccupied building has been dismantled under this Act, and shall entrust the competent registry office with cancelling the registration of that building.

Article 44 (Prevention of disasters in public buildings) (1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems it necessary to take measures to ensure the safety of a public building owned and managed by any of the following institutions in order to ensure the safety of the building from disasters, such as earthquakes and fires, he or she may request the manager of the relevant public building to improve the performance thereof; in such cases, the manager of the public building shall comply therewith unless there is a compelling reason not to do so:
  1. State agencies;
  2. Local governments;
  3. Public institutions;
  4. Local public enterprises established pursuant to the Local Public Enterprises Act;
  5. Other institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree to ensure public safety.
  (2) The manager of a public building shall notify the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport of the completion of performance improvement within 30 days from the date of completion thereof under paragraph (1).
  (3) Matters necessary for the subjects and procedures of performance improvement under paragraphs (1) and (2) and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.

Article 45 (Reporting and inspection) (1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu deems it necessary for the enforcement of this Act, he or she may require the manager to submit necessary data or report thereupon.
  (2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may require relevant public officials to enter the relevant building, etc. to inspect books, documents, and other matters where it is impracticable to achieve the purpose of investigation by submitting data or reports under paragraph (1).
  (3) Where an inspection is to be conducted pursuant to paragraph (2), a person subject to inspection shall be notified of the inspection plan, including the date and time of, grounds for, and the details of the inspection, 7 days prior to the inspection; provided, this shall not apply to urgent cases or where it is deemed impossible to attain the purpose of the inspection due to the destruction of evidence, etc., if given prior notice.
  (4) A public official who enters and inspects the relevant building pursuant to paragraph (2) shall show his or her certificate of authority to interested persons and he or she shall deliver a document indicating his or her name and the time and purpose of his or her visit at the time of his or her visit.

Article 46 (Accident investigation) (1) A manager shall take emergency safety measures without delay in cases of accident occurrence in a building under his or her jurisdiction, and where an accident exceeds the scale prescribed by Presidential Decree occurs, he or she shall notify the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu of the occurrence of such accident.
  (2) The Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu who is notified of the occurrence of an accident pursuant to paragraph (1) shall notify the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport thereof without delay.
  (3) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport is notified of the occurrence of an accident pursuant to paragraph (2), he or she may investigate the cause, etc. of the accident.
  (4) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may organize and operate the central building accident investigation committee if deemed necessary to investigate an accident, etc. of a building which has caused a greater loss than that prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (5) The Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may organize and operate a building accident investigation committee, if deemed necessary for investigating an accident, such as collapse, damage, etc. of a building under his or her jurisdiction.
  (6) The manager shall actively cooperate with the central building accident investigation committee under paragraph (4) or the building accident investigation committee under paragraph (5) in the preservation of the scene of an accident, the submission of data, the provision of relevant equipment, the hearing of opinions of the relevant persons, etc. necessary for investigating an accident.
  (7) Where the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu has conducted an accident investigation by the building accident investigation committee under paragraph (5), he or she shall notify the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport of the results thereof without delay.
  (8) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall publicly announce the results of an accident investigation by the central building accident investigation committee under paragraph (4) or the building accident investigation committee under paragraph (5).
  (9) Where necessary as a result of an accident investigation, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may order the relevant manager to take corrective measures, such as repair and reinforcement, if necessary.
  (10) The central building accident investigation committee under paragraph (4) and the building accident investigation committee under paragraph (5) may be integrated and operated with the central committee for investigating an accident involving an establishment or a committee for investigating an accident involving an establishment under Article 58 of the Special Act on the Safety Control and Maintenance of Establishments.
  (11) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport entrusts affairs concerning the operation of the central building accident investigation committee to an institution pursuant to Article 50 (2), he or she may provide contributions or subsidies to cover the expenses incurred in conducting such affairs to the relevant institution. <Added on Feb. 3, 2022>
  (12) Matters necessary for the composition and operation of the central building accident investigation committee under paragraph (4) or the building accident investigation committee under paragraph (5), the notification of accident investigations under paragraph (7), the public announcement of the results under paragraph (8), and other relevant matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>

Article 47 (Confidentiality) Any person who conducts inspection of building management and supervision of dismantlement works shall not divulge or appropriate any confidential information he or she has become aware of in the course of performing his or her duties; provided, this shall not apply where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems it necessary for the safety of buildings.

Article 48 (Hearings) Where a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu intends to render any of the following dispositions, he or she shall hold a hearing:
  1. Replacement of a building management inspection institution under Article 18 (5);
  2. Suspension of business of a building management inspection institution under Article 25;
  3. Replacement of supervisors of dismantlement works under Article 31 (2).

Article 49 (Legal fiction as public officials in applying penalty provisions) Any of the following persons shall be deemed to be a public official for the purposes of Articles 129 through 132 of the Criminal Act and penalty provisions under Articles 2 and 3 of the Act on the Aggravated Punishment of Specific Crimes: <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  1. A person who conducts building management inspections pursuant to Article 18 (1);
  2. Supervisors of dismantlement works;
  3. Executive officers and employees of building management support centers and local building management support centers under Articles 39 (1) and 40 (2);
  4. A member of the central building accident investigation committee under Article 46 (4) or a member of the building accident investigation committee under Article 46 (5);
  5. A member of an evaluation committee for building management inspections under Article 50 (3).

Article 50 (Delegation and entrustment of authority) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may delegate part of his or her authority under this Act to the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, or a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may entrust an institution with human resources and equipment necessary for performing the entrusted duties with the following authority among his or her authorities under this Act, as prescribed by Presidential Decree: <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  1. Management and operation of the building life cycle information system under Article 7;
  2. Education on conducting building management inspections under the provisions of Articles 13 through 16;
  3. Evaluation of results of building management inspections under Article 24 (1) and (2) and request for submission of relevant data necessary for the evaluation thereof.
  4. Accident investigations under Article 46;
  5. Affairs concerning the operation of the central building accident investigation committee under Article 46 (4).
  (3) An Institution entrusted with the authority to evaluate the results of building management inspections under paragraph (2) 3 shall establish an evaluation committee for building management inspections to ensure fairness and expertise in the evaluation and shall undergo deliberation thereon.
  (4) Necessary matters concerning the composition, operation, etc. of an evaluation committee of building management inspection under paragraph (3) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (5) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may contribute or subsidize some of the expenses incurred by the institutions under paragraph (2) in performing their duties.

CHAPTER VII PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 51 (Penalty provisions) (1) Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than 10 years or by a fine not exceeding 100 million won: <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  1. A person who causes a serious damage to a building, in violation of Article 12 (1), thereby posing a risk to the public;
  2. A person who causes a serious damage to a building by failing to conduct a periodic inspection under Article 13 (1), an emergency inspection under Article 14 (2), or a safety inspection under Article 16 (1) or failing to conduct a safety inspection faithfully, thereby posing a risk to the public;
  3. A person who causes a serious damage to a building by failing to conduct building management inspections or not conducting such inspections faithfully, in violation of Article 18 (4), thereby posing a risk to the public;
  4. A person who poses a risk to the public by failing to take measures, such as restrictions on use, prohibition of use, or dismantlement under Article 21 (1);
  5. A person who poses a risk to the public by failing to comply with an order issued under Article 21 (3) even after receiving such order;
  6. A person who causes a serious damage to a building by failing to take necessary measures, such as repair or reinforcement under Article 22 (1), thereby posing a risk to the public;
  7. A person who poses a risk to the public by failing to comply with an order issued under Article 24 (3) even after receiving such order;
  8. A person who causes danger to the public by failing to conduct the reinforcement of fire safety performance, in violation of Article 27 (2);
  9. A person who causes danger to the public while dismantlement of a building without obtaining permission for dismantlement of a building or with permission for dismantlement of a building obtained by fraud or other improper means, in violation of the main clause, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 30 (1) or Article 30 (2);
  10. A person who poses a risk to the public while dismantlement of a building without filing a report thereon or with a report thereon filed by fraud or other improper means, in violation of the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 30 (1);
  11. A person who causes a serious damage to a building by poorly preparing a dismantlement plan under Article 30 (4) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 30-3 (1)) or by preparing it in violation of this Act or relevant statutes or regulations, thereby posing a risk to the public;
  12. A person who causes a serious damage to a building by poorly reviewing a dismantlement plan under Article 30 (5) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 30-3 (1)) or by reviewing it in violation of this Act or relevant statutes or regulations, thereby posing a risk to the public;
  13. A person who poses a risk to the public while dismantlement of a building without filing a report on the commencement of dismantlement works or with a report thereon filed by fraud or other improper means, in violation of the proviso of Article 30-2 (1);
  14. A person who causes danger to the public while dismantlement of a building without obtaining permission for modification thereof or with permission therefor obtained by fraud or other improper means, in violation of Article 30-3 (1);
  15. A person who poses a risk to the public while dismantlement of a building without filing a report on modification thereof or with a report thereon filed by fraud or other improper means, in violation of Article 30-3 (1) or (2);
  16. A person who poses a risk to the public by failing to comply with an order issued by the permitting authority to take measures under Article 30-4 (2);
  17. A person who causes serious damage to a building by conducting any act falling under the subparagraphs of Article 31 (2), thereby causing danger to the public;
  18. A person who poses a risk to the public by failing to faithfully perform the supervision of dismantling works under Article 32 (1);
  19. A supervisor of dismantlement works who poses a risk to the public by failing to request correction or suspension of dismantlement works under Article 32 (2);
  20. A person who fails to comply with a request by a supervisor of dismantlement works to take corrective measures or who continues dismantlement works in defiance of the request to suspend the works, in violation of Article 32 (2), thereby posing a risk to the public;
  21. A person who poses a risk to the public by failing to faithfully perform the duties of dismantling workers, in violation of Article 32-2.
  (2) Any person who causes death or injury to a person in the course of committing a crime falling under the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for life at least 1 year.

Article 51-2 (Penalty provisions) Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than 2 years or by a fine not exceeding 20 million won:
  1. A person who conducts dismantlement works without obtaining permission for dismantlement of a building or with permission therefor obtained by fraud or other improper means, in violation of the main clause, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 30 (1) or Article 30 (2);
  2. A person who poorly prepares a dismantlement plan under Article 30 (4) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 30-3 (1)) or prepares it in violation of this Act or relevant statutes or regulations;
  3. A person who conducts dismantlement works without obtaining permission for modification thereof or with permission therefor obtained by fraud or other improper means, in violation of Article 30-3 (1);
  4. A person who fails to comply with an order issued by the permitting authority to take measures under Article 30-4 (2);
  5. A person who fails to comply with a request by a supervisor of dismantlement works to take corrective measures or who continues dismantlement works in defiance of the request to suspend the works, in violation of Article 32 (2);
  6. A person who fails to faithfully perform the duties of dismantling workers, in violation of Article 32-2.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 3, 2022]

Article 52 (Penalty provisions) Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than 1 year, or by a fine not exceeding 10 million won: <Amended on Jun. 9, 2020; Jul. 27, 2021; Feb. 3, 2022; Jun. 10, 2022>
  1. A person who violates Article 12 (1);
  2. A person who has been designated as a building management inspection institution under Article 18 (1) by fraud or other improper means;
  3. Any person who fails to comply with an order to implement or take corrective measures under Article 22 (2);
  4. A person who fails to comply with an order issued under Article 24 (3) even after receiving such order;
  5. A person who has received a disposition of business suspension under Article 25 (1) and newly conducts building management inspections during the period of business suspension;
  6. A person who fails to conduct the reinforcement of fire safety performance in violation of Article 27 (2) or fails to supplement the reinforcement of fire safety performance within the specified deadline after receiving an order for supplementation pursuant to Article 28 (6);
  7. A person who fails to file a report on dismantlement works of buildings or conducts dismantlement works with a report thereon filed by fraud or other improper means, in violation of the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 30 (1);
  8. A person who poorly reviews a dismantlement plan under Article 30 (5) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 30-3 (1)) or reviews it in violation of this Act or relevant statutes or regulations;
  9. A person who conducts dismantlement works without filing a report on the commencement of dismantlement works or with a report thereon filed by fraud or other improper means, in violation of the proviso of Article 30-2 (1);
  10. A person who fails to file a report on modification of dismantlement works or conducts dismantlement works with a report on modification thereof filed by fraud or other improper means, in violation of Article 30-3 (1) or (2);
  11. A person who conducts any act falling under Article 31 (2) 2;
  12. A person who fails to comply with the standards for placing construction inspectors to ensure the safety of dismantlement works of buildings without good cause, in violation of Article 31 (6);
  13. A supervisor of dismantlement works who fails to report to the permitting authority pursuant to Article 32 (3);
  14. A person who violates the order to take measures on buildings under Article 41 (1);
  15. A person who refuses, interferes with, or evades to report or conduct inspections under Article 45 (1) or (2);
  16. A person who fails to comply with the order to take measures under Article 46 (9);
  17. A person who divulges confidential information he or she has become aware of in the course of performing his or her duties, in violation of Article 47.

Article 53 (Joint penal provisions) Where the representative of a corporation, or an agent, employee, or any other servant of a corporation or individual commits an offence under Article 51, 51-2, or 52 in connection with the affairs of the corporation or the individual, not only shall such offender be punished, but also the corporation or the individual shall be punished by a fine under the relevant provisions; provided, this shall not apply where such corporation or individual has not been negligent in giving due attention and supervision concerning the relevant affairs to prevent such offence. <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>

Article 54 (Administrative fines) (1) Any of the following persons shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding 20 million won: <Added on Feb. 3, 2022>
  1. A person who conducts any act falling under Article 31 (2) 1, 3, or 4;
  2. A supervisor of dismantlement works who fails to faithfully perform the supervision of dismantlement works, in violation of Article 32 (1);
  3. A supervisor of dismantlement works who fails to request correction or suspension of dismantlement works under Article 32 (2);
  4. A person who fails to take photographs or record videos and store them under Article 32 (5).
  (2) Any of the following persons shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding 10 million won: <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  1. A person who fails to submit data under Article 6 (2) or submits false data;
  2. A person who fails to conduct a periodic inspection under Article 13 (1), an emergency inspection under Article 14 (2), or a safety inspection under Article 16 (1) or failing to conduct a safety inspection faithfully;
  3. A person who fails to perform the duties of building management inspections faithfully, in violation of Article 18 (4);
  4. A person who fails to comply with an order issued under Article 21 (3) even after receiving such order;
  5. A person who fails to take necessary measures, such as repair or reinforcement under Article 22 (1);
  6. A person who fails to inform people who use or utilize the relevant building with facts necessary for taking urgent measures, such as repair or reinforcement pursuant to Article 22 (3);
  7. A person who fails to report the results of reinforcement of fire safety performance or makes a false report thereon, in violation of Article 28 (3) and (6);
  8. A person who submits a dismantlement plan which is not formulated by a person falling under the subparagraphs of Article 30 (4) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 30-3 (1)) to the permitting authority;
  9. A person who submits a dismantlement plan which is not reviewed by a person falling under the subparagraphs of Article 30 (5) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 30-3 (1)) to the permitting authority;
  10. A person who fails to file a report on the results of an on-site inspection under Article 30-4 (4) or files a report thereon by fraud or other improper means;
  11. A person who fails to submit a report on the completion of supervision of dismantlement works under Article 32 (8);
  12. A person who fails to file a report on the completion of dismantlement works of a building under Article 33 (1);
  13. A person who fails to take emergency safety measures under Article 46 (1) or fails to notify the facts of the occurrence of an accident.
  (3) Any of the following persons shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding 5 million won: <Amended on Jul. 27, 2021; Feb. 3, 2022>
  1. A person who fails to file a report on the results of building management inspections under Article 20 (1) or files a report thereon by fraud or other improper means;
  2. A person who fails to submit data necessary for evaluating the results of building management inspections under Article 24 (2) or submits data thereon by fraud or other improper means;
  3. A person who fails to file a report on modification or files a report thereon by fraud or other improper means, in violation of Article 30-3 (3);
  4. A person who violates an order to report or conduct inspections under Article 45 (1) or (2).
  (4) Any of the following persons shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding 2 million won: <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>
  1. A person who fails to record, store, or maintain information related to building management, such as inspection, repair, or reinforcement of buildings, pursuant to Article 10 (1);
  2. A person who fails to formulate or submit a building management plan, in violation of Article 11 (1);
  3. A person who fails to replace or repair important facilities pursuant to building management plans formulated or modified in violation of Article 11 (5);
  4. A person who fails to enter the results of measures taken in the building life cycle information system, in violation of Article 11 (6);
  5. A person who fails to submit a safety inspection report, in violation of Article 16 (5);
  6. A person who fails to verify whether matters under Article 20 (2) are implemented;
  7. A person who fails to file a report on the results of measures, such as repair or reinforcement, taken in violation of Article 23 (1);
  8. A person who fails to file a report on destruction of buildings, in violation of Article 34 (1).
  (5) Administrative fines under the provisions of paragraphs (1) through (4) shall be imposed and collected by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, or a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Feb. 3, 2022>

